        "result": "tmux, or \"terminal multiplexer,\" is a tool that lets users create, manage, and navigate multiple terminal sessions within a single window. It solves a fundamental problem for developers and system administrators: the need to run several programs, monitor logs, and juggle SSH connections without opening dozens of separate terminal windows. At its core, tmux organizes work into sessions, which contain windows, which in turn can be split into panes—each pane running its own independent shell. This hierarchy makes it easy to organize complex workflows, such as editing code in one pane while running tests in another and monitoring server output in a third. One of tmux's most valued features is session persistence: a session keeps running on the server even after you disconnect, so you can reattach later—whether from the same machine or a different one—and pick up exactly where you left off. This makes it indispensable for remote work over SSH, where network interruptions would otherwise kill long-running processes. tmux is highly configurable, supporting custom key bindings, status bars, and scriptable automation, and it integrates well with tools like vim and shell scripts, making it a staple in many command-line-centric workflows.",
