        "result": "tmux, short for \"terminal multiplexer,\" is a powerful command-line tool that allows users to run and manage multiple terminal sessions from a single window, dramatically improving productivity for developers, system administrators, and anyone who spends significant time in a shell environment. At its core, tmux introduces a hierarchical structure of sessions, windows, and panes: a session can contain multiple windows (similar to tabs), and each window can be split into several panes, each running an independent shell process. This structure allows users to organize complex workflows visually and logically—for instance, editing source code in one pane, running a build or test suite in another, and tailing application logs in a third, all visible simultaneously without switching between separate terminal applications.\n\nOne of tmux's most transformative features is session persistence. Unlike a standard terminal window, a tmux session continues running on the host machine even after the user disconnects, whether due to a closed SSH connection, network interruption, or simply closing a laptop lid. Users can detach from a session and later reattach to it—potentially from an entirely different machine—and find all their programs, processes, and pane arrangements exactly as they left them. This makes tmux especially valuable for remote server administration, long-running scripts, and any workflow where connection stability cannot be guaranteed.\n\ntmux is also highly customizable. Its default keybindings can be remapped, its status bar can display custom information like system load or git branch, and its behavior can be extended through configuration files and plugins. Many users integrate tmux tightly with editors like vim or neovim, using matching navigation keybindings to move seamlessly between editor splits and terminal panes. Additionally, tmux supports scripting and automation, allowing entire complex session layouts to be created programmatically at startup. Combined with its lightweight resource footprint and broad availability across Unix-like systems, tmux has become a foundational tool in many command-line-centric development and operations workflows, prized for the control and continuity it brings to terminal-based work.",
